libjava: use bionic's strerror_r, and take its return type

Two things had to change together, and the first alone left the build worse
than before.

getErrorString() wants the XSI strerror_r that returns int. Under _GNU_SOURCE
glibc supplies the GNU one returning char*, so the file redirects the call to
glibc's internal __xpg_strerror_r. The build defines _GNU_SOURCE for linux, so
android took that path and libjava did not link:

  ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: __xpg_strerror_r

Dropping the redirect exposed the reason it existed: bionic follows glibc here,
so under _GNU_SOURCE its strerror_r returns char* too, and the function does
not compile:

  jni_util_md.c:73:12: error: incompatible pointer to integer conversion
      returning 'char * _Nonnull' from a function with result type 'int'

So handle the GNU form on android: copy the message into buf when the call
returns a static string, and report success the way the XSI call would.

termux-packages changes getErrorString's signature to char* instead
(0029-libjava-change-return-type-of-strerror_r). Keeping int avoids touching
jni_util.h and every caller; both callers in 8 ignore the return value and read
buf -- io_util_md.c takes strlen(buf), getLastErrorString checks buf[0].

diff --git a/jdk/src/solaris/native/common/jni_util_md.c b/jdk/src/solaris/native/common/jni_util_md.c
index 598dc41..154afa3 100644
--- a/jdk/src/solaris/native/common/jni_util_md.c
+++ b/jdk/src/solaris/native/common/jni_util_md.c
@@ -37,7 +37,7 @@ char* nativeGetStringPlatformChars(JNIEnv *env, jstring jstr, jboolean *isCopy)
     return NULL;
 }
 
-#if defined(LINUX) && (defined(_GNU_SOURCE) || \
+#if defined(LINUX) && !defined(__ANDROID__) && (defined(_GNU_SOURCE) || \
          (defined(_POSIX_C_SOURCE) && _POSIX_C_SOURCE < 200112L \
              && defined(_XOPEN_SOURCE) && _XOPEN_SOURCE < 600))
 extern int __xpg_strerror_r(int, char *, size_t);
@@ -70,5 +70,19 @@ int
 getErrorString(int err, char *buf, size_t len)
 {
     if (err == 0 || len < 1) return 0;
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+    /* bionic follows glibc here: under _GNU_SOURCE strerror_r returns char*,
+     * and may hand back a static string instead of filling buf. Copy it in and
+     * report success, which is what the XSI call would have done. */
+    {
+        char *msg = strerror_r(err, buf, len);
+        if (msg != buf) {
+            strncpy(buf, msg, len - 1);
+            buf[len - 1] = '\0';
+        }
+        return 0;
+    }
+#else
     return strerror_r(err, buf, len);
+#endif
 }
